.TH TRESTLED 8 "October 2026" "trestle 0.1" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
trestled \- privileged daemon of the trestle container network plugin
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trestled
.RB [ \-c
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-t ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trestled
runs as root and performs the network work requested by the
.BR trestle (8)
client on behalf of CNI runtimes and Netavark, including rootless Podman.
It listens on a unix socket (default \fI/tmp/trestle.sock\fR; Netavark runs
plugins with a private \fI/run\fR, so \fI/tmp\fR is used). At start-up any
file already present at the socket path is removed, and the socket is
recreated if it is later deleted or replaced. Its owner, group and mode come
from the configuration (default root:root 0777).
.PP
Each request is handled as follows:
.IP 1. 4
The caller's uid and pid are read with \fBSO_PEERCRED\fR and the user name is
looked up. The request is parsed and validated again (arguments, \fBCNI_*\fR
variables, JSON document).
.IP 2. 4
\fI/proc/<pid>/exe\fR of the caller must be the configured
\fBclient_binary\fR.
.IP 3. 4
The network must have a \fB[network:NAME]\fR section and the user must be
allowed by its \fBusers\fR list.
.IP 4. 4
The bridge (\fBtbr\fR + first 12 hex digits of md5(network) unless
\fBbridge\fR is set) is created if missing, brought up, given the
\fBsubnet\fR address, MTU, STP / VLAN filtering settings and the optional
\fBuplink\fR.
.IP 5. 4
For CNI ADD / Netavark setup a veth pair is created. The host side is named
\fBtrv\fR + first 12 hex digits of md5("\fInetwork\fR:\fIuser\fR:\fIcontainer\fR"),
gets the alias \fBtrestle:\fIuser\fB.\fIcontainer\fB.\fInetwork\fR and is
enslaved to the bridge. The peer is created directly inside the container
network namespace (received as a file descriptor for rootless callers) with
the interface name requested by the runtime. An address from the subnet and
a default route via the bridge are configured.
.IP 6. 4
Names \fIuser\fR.\fIcontainer\fR.\fInetwork\fR.trestle are published in
\fI/etc/hosts\fR, a dnsmasq hosts file and/or aardvark-dns, as configured.
.PP
DEL / teardown reverse these steps. A bridge is deleted only when trestle
created it, no ports other than the configured uplink remain, and it is not
claimed by NetworkManager, systemd-networkd or libvirt.
.PP
Leases and attachment records are kept in \fI/var/lib/trestle\fR, so the
daemon can be restarted at any time without affecting running containers.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c ", \-\-config " file
Configuration file (default \fI/etc/trestle/trestle.conf\fR). Drop-ins are
read from the \fIconf.d\fR directory next to it.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-foreground
Log to standard error. Under systemd messages always go to the journal with
proper priorities.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Debug logging (overrides \fBlog_level\fR).
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-test
Check the configuration, print the networks and the generated nftables
ruleset, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the configuration, re-apply nftables rules, socket permissions and the
managed \fI/etc/hosts\fR block. An invalid configuration is rejected and the
running one is kept.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Remove the socket and (with \fBfirewall_cleanup\fR) the nftables table, then
exit. Containers stay connected.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/trestle/trestle.conf
.TP
.I /etc/trestle/conf.d/*.conf
.TP
.I /var/lib/trestle/
leases (\fIipam/\fR), attachments, bridge ownership markers.
.TP
.I /run/trestle/aardvark-dns/
aardvark-dns configuration for networks with \fBaardvark_dns = yes\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR trestle (8),
.BR trestle.conf (5),
.BR nft (8),
.BR systemctl (1)
